# Secure coding (baseline)

Portable defaults. Prefer the project's existing auth, crypto, and validation
libraries — do not invent a second stack. Deeper checklist: skill
`code-security`.

## Secrets and sensitive data

- Never hardcode passwords, API keys, tokens, private keys, or connection
  strings in source, tests, fixtures, docs, or commits
- Load secrets from the environment, a secret store, or the project's approved
  config path — ship examples with placeholders only
- Do not log secrets, session tokens, password hashes, or full auth headers
- Redact or omit sensitive fields in error messages returned to clients

## Untrusted input

- Treat all external input as untrusted: HTTP bodies/headers/query, CLI args,
  file uploads, webhooks, message queues, third-party APIs, and user-stored data
  read back later
- Validate type, length, range, and allowlists at the trust boundary before use
- Encode/escape for the sink (HTML, SQL, shell, URL, LDAP) — do not concatenate
  untrusted strings into queries, commands, or templates
- Prefer parameterized queries / bound arguments for databases; never build SQL
  with string interpolation of user data
- Prefer argv arrays / library APIs over shelling out; if a shell is unavoidable,
  pass a fixed command and validated arguments only

## Authentication and authorization

- Deny by default; grant the minimum role/permission required
- Check authorization on every sensitive action server-side — never trust
  client-only flags, hidden fields, or “disabled” UI as enforcement
- Use the project's session/token primitives; do not roll custom crypto for
  passwords or tokens when a maintained library exists
- On failed authz, return a generic denial without leaking whether a resource
  exists when that distinction matters

## Output and side effects

- Escape untrusted data in HTML/JS contexts; do not render raw HTML unless a
  deliberate sanitizer is already the project standard
- Scope filesystem paths to an allowlisted root; reject `..` and absolute paths
  from user input
- Do not follow user-supplied URLs/hosts for server-side fetches without an
  allowlist (SSRF)
- Default new network listeners and CORS/origins to the narrowest correct set

## Dependencies and crypto

- Prefer well-maintained libraries for TLS, password hashing, JWT, and OAuth
- Use proven constructions (e.g. salted password hashes via the platform
  standard); never invent hashing/encryption schemes
- Prefer cryptographically secure randomness for tokens, session IDs, and
  secrets — not `rand` / non-crypto PRNGs

## When this change is high risk

If the edit touches auth, sessions, crypto, payments, multi-tenant isolation,
SQL, command execution, file uploads, or public network surfaces: load skill
`code-security` and run its checklist before calling the work done.
